#: ../../apps/site/src/components/landing/FAQSection.tsx:60
#~ msgid "Aziru is named after the Amarna letters, a cache of clay tablets from around 1350 BC holding the diplomatic correspondence between the pharaoh of Egypt and the kings and vassals of the ancient Near East. They are one of the earliest surviving inboxes: a ruler working through hundreds of incoming messages, deciding what needed a reply and what could wait. Tellingly, almost all of it is inbound. We have hundreds of letters to the pharaoh but barely any of his replies, and senders like Rib-Hadda of Byblos wrote again and again without an answer, most likely buried under an inbox no one could keep up with. Aziru spares you that fate."
#~ msgstr ""

